{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  "tasks": {
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", ".next/**", "!.next/cache/**"],
      // Turbo 2 runs tasks in STRICT env mode: anything not listed here is
      // stripped from the task's environment. Both of these are consumed by
      // `next build` (next.config.ts) — BFF_URL is serialised into the routes
      // manifest as the /graphql rewrite target, and omitting it here is
      // exactly how the web image silently proxied to itself (found by the
      // M8 PR5 browser verification). Declaring them also makes them part of
      // the task hash, so a changed BFF_URL correctly busts the cache.
      "env": ["BFF_URL", "NEXT_STANDALONE"]
    },
    "typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"]
    },
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "env": ["PG_TEST_URL", "CI"]
    },
    "dev": {
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": true
    },

    /*
     * TWO PACKAGES BUILD SOMEWHERE OTHER THAN `dist`, AND SAID SO NOWHERE.
     *
     * An undeclared output is not cached — and worse, a cache HIT then skips
     * the build and leaves that directory absent, so turbo reports a package
     * successfully built that is missing half of itself. The failure is silent
     * in the direction that matters.
     *
     * Found by building the repo in a fresh git worktree (M16 PR2a): turbo
     * restored `@estate/vault-crypto`'s `dist`, skipped the build because the
     * hash matched, and `dist-esm` — the BROWSER build that `vault-web` copies
     * into its served tree — simply did not exist. `vault-web`'s copy step
     * failed with ENOENT against a dependency turbo considered done.
     *
     * `vault-web` has the same shape twice over: it compiles its browser client
     * into `public/app` and receives vault-crypto's modules in `public/lib`.
     * Both are gitignored build output, so a restored cache would have served
     * the Zone A origin with no client at all.
     *
     * Declared per package rather than globally, so these paths cannot start
     * meaning something in a package that happens to have a `public/`
     * directory of checked-in assets — which `apps/web` does.
     */
    "@estate/vault-crypto#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", "dist-esm/**"]
    },
    "@estate/vault-web#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", "public/app/**", "public/lib/**"]
    },
    // The operator origin has the same shape as `vault-web`, minus the copied
    // crypto: one package emitting an edge into `dist` and a browser client
    // into `public/app`. Declared for the reason above rather than for caching
    // — an undeclared output makes a cache HIT serve the origin with no client
    // at all, which boots, renders the shell, and answers 200 at `/`.
    "@estate/operator-web#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", "public/app/**"]
    },

    /*
     * AND THE EXTENSION BAKES AN ORIGIN, so strict env mode was silently
     * discarding it — the same defect as BFF_URL above, in the same file, one
     * milestone later.
     *
     * MEASURED, not inferred: `VAULT_ORIGIN=https://vault.example.test pnpm
     * build --filter=@estate/vault-extension --force` produced a package whose
     * manifest and `origin.js` both said `http://vault.localhost:3010`. The
     * build script validates the value it reads and throws on a bad one, so it
     * cannot catch this — it never saw the variable at all and fell through to
     * its dev default. Exit 0, green everywhere, wrong artifact.
     *
     * Declaring it here also puts it in the task HASH, which the caching half
     * of this needs: without that, a build for one origin would be served from
     * the cache of a build for another.
     *
     * `OUT_DIR` is declared for the same rule rather than because turbo passes
     * it — only the specs set it, to build into a temp directory. The fence in
     * `test/turbo-env.spec.ts` derives this list from the script's own
     * `process.env` reads, so the next build-time input is declared or the
     * build goes red.
     */
    "@estate/vault-extension#build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**"],
      "env": ["VAULT_ORIGIN", "OUT_DIR"]
    }
  }
}
